\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\paragraph{Predictor Shift Mechanism.}
At $K{=}3$, a majority requires $t{=}2$; a single compromised judge can flip the panel, making both $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ relevant (co-determination: 3/12 and 4/12 under bootstrap). At $K{\geq}5$, the adversary must compromise ${\geq}3$ judges; high-$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ panels present more distinct attack surfaces, and $\eta_{\max}$'s marginal contribution collapses because even high-$\eta$ judges cannot flip the panel alone. The transition threshold $K^*$ lies between 3 and 5.

\paragraph{Uniform Positive Direction.}
$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s coefficient direction is positive in all 12 conditions under both OLS and GAM bootstrap (88.9--100\% positive rate at $K{=}3$, 100\% at $K{=}5$); statistical significance depends on inference method (OLS 12/12; wild cluster bootstrap 4/12 at $K{=}3$, 10/12 at $K{=}5$). A Kruskal--Wallis test finds no significant magnitude difference between targeted and shared-bias attacks ($p{=}0.200$ at $K{=}3$, $p{=}0.423$ at $K{=}5$; Appendix~\ref{app:bft_reversal}), ruling out attack-mechanism-dependent directional structure.

\paragraph{Diversity--Capability Confound.}
$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ are correlated at $r{=}0.70$ ($K{=}3$) and $r{=}0.81$ ($K{=}5$; Figure~\ref{fig:keff_eta_scatter}), reflecting a structural property: maximizing diversity requires sampling across capability tiers, introducing weaker judges (Appendix~\ref{app:diversity_capability}). Cinelli--Hazlett sensitivity analysis \citep{cinelli2020making} shows the known $\eta_{\max}$ confound exceeds $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s robustness value in 28/36 conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity}), with robustness increasing with $K$ (1/12 $\to$ 3/12 $\to$ 4/12). The API-only subset ($r{=}0.85$) does not replicate the transition (3/12). Capability-matched subsampling ($r{=}0.50$, 9 models, 126 panels at $K{=}5$; Figure~\ref{fig:keff_eta_scatter}, orange) provides partial mitigation: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ strengthens to 9/12 and RV exceeds $\eta_{\max}$ in 10/12 conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity_poolA}), versus 3/12 in the full pool. The transition is thus a descriptive regime shift whose causal attribution to diversity requires capability-controlled designs.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_keff_eta_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Panel-level correlation between $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ at $K{=}5$ ($n{=}3{,}003$ panels). Full model pool (gray, $r{=}0.81$) exhibits strong collinearity; capability-matched Pool~A (orange diamonds, $r{=}0.50$, 126 panels) shows reduced confounding.}
\label{fig:keff_eta_scatter}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Aggregation and Deployment.}
Aggregation rules modulate how much variance panel composition explains rather than the direction of $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s association. Threshold voting yields $R^2{=}0.829$ (near-deterministic); Bayesian calibration reduces $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance (5+/1$-$) and overall predictability ($R^2{=}0.087$), but $\eta_{\max}$ persists (9/12; Table~\ref{tab:aggregation}). Robustness-first selection ($\min \eta_{\max}$) achieves lowest ASR in 12/12 conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:panel_selection}). Recomputing MI under attack confirms stability ($\rho{\geq}0.91$ for 4/6 attacks; position bias disrupts MI on ARC, $\rho{=}0.08$; Appendix~\ref{app:attacked_keff}). The random-fault contrast (\S\ref{sec:rf_contrast}) confirms $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ predicts under non-adaptive noise ($R^2 = 0.73$--$0.81$), with the predictability collapse most pronounced at $K{=}3$.

\section{Experimental Methodology}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Models and Panel Construction}

We select 15 LLM judges spanning 6 provider families and 3 capability tiers, listed in Table~\ref{tab:models}. Nine models are accessed via the OpenRouter API; six open-weight models are served locally using vLLM~0.21.0 with AWQ INT4 quantization on an NVIDIA RTX~6000 Ada (48\,GB). API models are assigned to \emph{frontier}, \emph{mid-tier}, or \emph{lightweight} tiers following the provider capability classification; all six local models fall into the lightweight tier, as INT4 quantization substantially reduces judge accuracy on the pairwise judgment task (Table~\ref{tab:models}). We study three panel sizes $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$ (all odd, majority-vote threshold $t = \lceil K/2 \rceil$). Exhaustive enumeration yields $\binom{15}{3} = 455$ panels at $K{=}3$, $\binom{15}{5} = 3{,}003$ at $K{=}5$, and $\binom{15}{7} = 6{,}435$ at $K{=}7$, covering every combination of families and tiers. Each panel evaluates all benchmark items under all attack types, producing a complete panel $\times$ item $\times$ attack tensor. The primary analysis uses $K{=}3$; \S\ref{sec:cross_k} extends to larger panels.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
