\subsection{Panel-Size-Dependent Predictor Shift}
\label{sec:cross_k}

Table~\ref{tab:method_comparison} reveals that the $K{=}3$ significance pattern is method-sensitive: OLS finds both predictors significant (co-dominant in 10/12 conditions), clustered SE favors $\eta_{\max}$ (8/12 vs.\ 1/12), wild bootstrap shows weak significance for both (3/12 vs.\ 4/12), and fractional logit with clustered SE gives $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 9/12. The method sensitivity at $K{=}3$ contrasts with $K{\geq}5$, where $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s dominance is consistent across methods.

A natural question is whether this pattern persists at larger panel sizes. We address this by repeating the wild cluster bootstrap analysis at $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$, constructing all $\binom{15}{K}$ panels for each value and applying the same specification (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}) with $K$-position max-$p$ correction.

